on requests are expected to grow 9%, which is slower than in 2026. Utilities are 62% of VLTX holdings' orders. Pellham is 35% of those and Norvane 21%.\n- **Financing:** Funding is only partly there.\n  - Norvane priced a C$1.1B bond comfortably, so it looks able to fund its plan.\n  - Pellham withdrew a C$2.4B equity offering after demand fell short. It is cutting 2027 transformer orders by about 40% and deferring two substation programmes.\n  - Utility bond spreads widened 30bp to 165bp in Q3, and two other utility share offerings were downsized.\n  - Pellham is VLTX's largest single customer, so the headline +18% overstates the orders VLTX is likely to see.\n- **Pricing:** The LEAPS are fairly priced, not cheap. Implied volatility is 27% against a 2-year median of 26%, and skew is flat. The data didn't include strikes, expiries, premiums or the underlying price. I couldn't check any specific contract.\n\n**What would make me add:**\n- Utility spreads tightening back toward about 135bp.\n- Pellham returning with a debt or hybrid deal, or restoring the deferred programmes.\n- A dip in the options' implied volatility.\n\nIf you add anyway, a call spread would limit the cost of volatility exposure.\n\nThe evidence is thin, covering two utilities and a handful of short documents. Refs: n2.r1, n3.r1, n4.r1.","ttft_ms":2596,"type":"result","duration_ms":33716,"uuid":"25bfcb7d-e4c5-47b3-a08b-bfc0c4152049","ttft_stream_ms":1111,"time_to_request_ms":143,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":4,"system_prompt":9,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":5,"input_attachments":17,"input_hooks":4,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":18,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":21,"init_frame_send":5,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":4,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":4,"autocompact":5,"request_announcements":12,"query_setup":1,"model_gates":4,"tool_schema_build":5,"message_normalization":6,"client_creation":6,"request_build":9},"turn_start_resume_kind":"none","process_turn_index